# Deny everything, then name what the build needs.
#
# An allowlist rather than a list of exclusions, and that is the whole
# point: this image is published, and the repo it is built from contains
# license-validator/.env (R2 write credentials, the admin token), a root
# .env (a customer licence key) and dist/krea2app (the licensed binary).
# A deny-list is one forgotten line away from shipping one of them; a file
# that is not listed below cannot reach the build context at all.
#
# The Dockerfile also copies the thirteen ember/ files the bake stage
# imports into a throwaway build stage rather than the final image, so none
# of the app's source is published either — see the stage comment there.

*

# Pins and the mirror index — bake_nodes.py resolves every revision from
# these two files.
!scripts/PINS.json
!scripts/mirror_manifest.json

# What bake_nodes.py imports to read them. Build stage only.
!ember/__init__.py
!ember/features.py
!ember/logs.py
!ember/settings.py
!ember/weights/__init__.py
!ember/weights/mirror.py
!ember/comfy/__init__.py
!ember/comfy/setup.py
!ember/pipelines/__init__.py
!ember/pipelines/krea2/__init__.py
!ember/pipelines/krea2/constants.py
!ember/pipelines/krea2_v2/__init__.py
!ember/pipelines/krea2_v2/constants.py

# The start script, copied in verbatim: the same file build.sh publishes
# to R2 and a RunPod template downloads from /v1/start.sh.
!scripts/runpod_start.sh

!docker/bake_nodes.py
!docker/bake_torch.py
!docker/entrypoint.sh

# The dev image (docker/Dockerfile.dev) installs the app's own dependencies
# so a mounted source tree can run. Only the requirements file is needed —
# the source is bind-mounted at run time, never copied into an image.
!requirements.txt
!docker/Dockerfile.dev

# Junk inside the directories allowed above. Last match wins, so these
# have to come after the exceptions.
**/__pycache__
**/*.py[cod]
**/.git
**/.gitignore

# webui/ and ember/web/webui_bundle.py are NOT listed above, and that is
# deliberate.
#
# This image is the ENVIRONMENT — ComfyUI, its custom nodes and their
# pinned revisions — not the app. The binary that serves the React UI is
# built by build.sh / build.ps1 and published to R2; nothing in this image
# ever imports ember/web/webui_bundle.py or reads webui/dist.
#
# Allowlisting webui/ would start publishing the front-end SOURCE into a
# public image, which is exactly what the deny-all above exists to
# prevent. Adding ember/web/webui_bundle.py alone would publish a few
# hundred KB of compiled JavaScript that nothing in the image can serve.
